export const TERMS_HTML = "<div class=\"ube-legal-masthead\"><p><strong>TERMS OF USE</strong></p>\n<p><strong>UBE Safety</strong></p>\n<p>Operated by <span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[Company Legal Name]</span> (Registration No. <span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[\u2022]</span>) (\u201cthe Company\u201d, \u201cwe\u201d, \u201cus\u201d, or \u201cour\u201d)</p>\n<p>Effective Date: 15 September 2026 | Last Updated: 1 September 2026</p>\n<p><strong>DRAFT FOR REVIEW \u2014 NOT YET APPROVED</strong></p>\n<p><em>This document was drafted with AI assistance as a starting point. It has not been reviewed by a South African attorney and must not be published or relied upon as the Company\u2019s live Terms of Use until it has been. See the accompanying note for specific open items.</em></p>\n</div><h2>1. Important Notice \u2014 Please Read First</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>If you are in immediate danger, contact SAPS on 10111, or 112 from a mobile phone, right now. Do not rely on this Service in an emergency.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li><p>This Service lets people submit and search structured safety-related reports about specific incidents. We do not verify whether any report is true.</p></li>\n<li><p>A report appearing about someone is not proof of guilt. No report appearing about someone is not proof that they are safe.</p></li>\n<li><p>We are not a law-enforcement agency, a court, or an emergency service, and using this Service is not the same as reporting a crime to the police.</p></li>\n<li><p>We act only as a neutral host for content that Users submit. We do not write, verify, or endorse it, and our review of it (moderation) does not make it true.</p></li>\n<li><p>By using this Service \u2014 including submitting a report without creating an account \u2014 you agree to the full Terms below, including limits on our responsibility and your own responsibility for what you submit.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>2. Acceptance of These Terms</h2>\n<p>These Terms of Use (\u201cTerms\u201d) govern your access to and use of UBE Safety (the \u201cService\u201d), operated by <span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[Company Legal Name]</span>. They apply to every User of the Service, including a Reporter who creates an Account, a Reporter who uses the Verified-Email or Anonymous submission channel described in Section 7, a Reported Individual who registers to lodge an appeal, and a Moderator.</p>\n<p>By accessing, browsing, creating an account, submitting a report, uploading evidence, or otherwise using the Service in any way, you agree to be bound by these Terms and by our Privacy Policy <span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[link]</span>, which is incorporated into these Terms by reference. If you do not agree to these Terms, you must not use the Service.</p>\n<p>Where you use the Service without creating an account, your acceptance is recorded at the point you click to submit a report or otherwise take an action that these Terms are presented alongside. The Company must ensure this acceptance step is a genuine, logged action and not merely a link in a footer \u2014 see the implementation note accompanying this document.</p>\n<h2>3. Eligibility</h2>\n<p>You must be at least 18 years old and have the legal capacity to enter into a binding agreement under the law of the Republic of South Africa to use this Service. The Service is not directed at, and must not be used by, anyone under 18.</p>\n<p>If the Company becomes aware that a User is under 18, the Company may suspend or terminate that User\u2019s access and delete their account, without prejudice to any retention of Content already submitted that the Company is required or entitled to keep.</p>\n<p><strong>Reports concerning a minor.</strong> Where a report concerns an incident in which the person being described, the reporter, or a witness is a minor, the Company may decline to process that report through its ordinary moderation channel and may instead direct the person submitting it toward appropriate child-protection authorities, a parent or guardian, or law enforcement. This reflects the additional legal protections that apply to information about minors under South African law and is not a refusal to take the matter seriously.</p>\n<h2>4. Definitions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>\u201cService\u201d means the website, application, and related functionality operated by the Company under <span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[domain]</span>.</p></li>\n<li><p>\u201cUser\u201d or \u201cyou\u201d means anyone who accesses or uses the Service in any capacity.</p></li>\n<li><p>\u201cReport\u201d means any structured submission describing an alleged incident, together with any supporting evidence attached to it.</p></li>\n<li><p>\u201cContent\u201d means Reports, evidence, appeals, moderator notes made visible to Users, and any other material submitted through the Service.</p></li>\n<li><p>\u201cReported Individual\u201d means a natural person who is named or otherwise identified, directly or indirectly, in a Report.</p></li>\n<li><p>\u201cModerator\u201d means a person authorised by the Company to review, restrict, label, or remove Content.</p></li>\n<li><p>\u201cPOPIA\u201d means the Protection of Personal Information Act 4 of 2013.</p></li>\n<li><p>\u201cECT Act\u201d means the Electronic Communications and Transactions Act 25 of 2002.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>5. Nature of the Service \u2014 We Are an Intermediary, Not a Publisher or Investigator</h2>\n<p>The Service allows Users to submit, store, and \u2014 subject to moderation \u2014 share structured Reports and supporting evidence about specific incidents, to help other Users make more informed personal-safety decisions.</p>\n<p>The Company does not investigate incidents, does not verify the truth of any Report, does not determine legal guilt or innocence, and is not a law-enforcement, judicial, or regulatory body.</p>\n<p>All Content is generated by Users, not by the Company. Where the Company or a Moderator reviews, categorises, labels (for example, as \u201cdisputed\u201d, \u201cunder review\u201d, or \u201cremoved\u201d), restricts, or removes Content, this is solely to operate a moderation process. It does not mean the Company adopts, endorses, verifies, or vouches for the truth of that Content.</p>\n<p>The Company\u2019s role in respect of Content submitted by Users is that of an intermediary that hosts material at a User\u2019s direction. Nothing in these Terms should be read as the Company authoring, initiating, selecting the recipients of, or modifying the substance of any Report. See Section 17 for how this applies to the Company\u2019s liability.</p>\n<h2>6. Not Emergency Services, Law Enforcement, or Professional Advice</h2>\n<p>The Service is not monitored in real time and must never be used to summon emergency help. If you or anyone else is in immediate danger, contact SAPS on 10111, 112 from a mobile phone, or another appropriate emergency service immediately.</p>\n<p>The Service is not a substitute for reporting a crime to SAPS, applying for a protection order, or seeking legal, medical, or psychological advice or treatment. Nothing on the Service constitutes legal, medical, psychological, or safety advice specific to your situation.</p>\n<p>A Report submitted through the Service, and any status shown on it (for example, that a case reference number was supplied), is not a police case, is not verified against any SAPS or judicial database, and does not create a legal record equivalent to a police report.</p>\n<h2>7. Accounts and Submission Channels</h2>\n<p>The Service allows a Report to be submitted through one of three channels, and you choose which applies each time you submit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Account \u2014 you are signed in, and the Report is linked to your account. You are responsible for maintaining the confidentiality of your login credentials and for all activity that occurs under your account.</p></li>\n<li><p>Verified Email \u2014 you provide an email address that is confirmed at the time of submission, without creating a full account. You are responsible for the accuracy of that email address and for retaining any recovery code provided to you.</p></li>\n<li><p>Anonymous \u2014 you provide no contact information at all. A one-time recovery code is shown to you when you submit; you are solely responsible for saving it. The Company cannot recover a lost recovery code, cannot verify your identity through any other means for a Report submitted this way, and cannot notify you of any update to the Report.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Company may suspend, limit, or terminate access to any submission channel, or to a specific User, at its discretion, including where it suspects abuse of the Verified Email or Anonymous channels.</p>\n<h2>8. User Content \u2014 Ownership, Licence, and Responsibility</h2>\n<p>You retain ownership of the Content you submit.</p>\n<p>By submitting Content, you grant the Company a worldwide, royalty-free, non-exclusive licence to host, store, reproduce, and adapt that Content (for example, to redact, format, or translate it) solely as necessary to operate, moderate, and improve the Service, including making it visible \u2014 subject to its moderation status and the Service\u2019s access rules \u2014 to Moderators and, where applicable, to a Reported Individual exercising an appeal.</p>\n<p>You are solely responsible for the accuracy, legality, and consequences of Content you submit. By submitting Content, you warrant that:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>it is true and accurate to the best of your knowledge;</p></li>\n<li><p>you have the legal right to submit any evidence you upload, including the right to record, hold, or share it under applicable law;</p></li>\n<li><p>you are not submitting it primarily to harass, defame, intimidate, or extort another person; and</p></li>\n<li><p>it does not violate any law or infringe any third party\u2019s rights.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Company has no obligation to publish, act on, retain, or verify any Content, and may refuse, edit (including to redact material), restrict, or remove Content at its sole discretion, before or after moderation.</p>\n<h2>9. Prohibited Conduct</h2>\n<p>In addition to any other restriction in these Terms, you must not use the Service to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>submit a Report you know, or reasonably suspect, to be false, exaggerated, or misleading;</p></li>\n<li><p>harass, stalk, threaten, dox, intimidate, or incite violence against any person, including a Reported Individual;</p></li>\n<li><p>publish another person\u2019s private information (for example, a home address, identity number, or financial details) beyond what is necessary and permitted for a Report;</p></li>\n<li><p>impersonate another person or misrepresent your affiliation with any person or entity;</p></li>\n<li><p>upload content that violates a third party\u2019s intellectual property or privacy rights, or that was obtained through unlawful surveillance;</p></li>\n<li><p>use automated means (bots, scrapers) to access, search, or submit to the Service without the Company\u2019s prior written consent;</p></li>\n<li><p>attempt to circumvent moderation, rate-limiting, or security controls; or</p></li>\n<li><p>use the Service for any purpose prohibited by South African law, including defamation, crimen injuria, or conduct addressed by the Domestic Violence Act, the Protection from Harassment Act, or the Cybercrimes Act.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Content involving minors.</strong> Any content depicting or sexualising a minor will be removed and reported to the relevant authorities without notice to the person who submitted it, and the associated account (if any) will be permanently terminated, regardless of any other provision of these Terms.</p>\n<h2>10. No Verification; No Warranty of Accuracy or Safety</h2>\n<p>A Report appearing on the Service is not proof that any statement in it is true, and is not evidence that any person is guilty of anything. The absence of a Report about a person is not proof, and must never be relied upon as proof, that the person is safe.</p>\n<p>The Company makes no representation or warranty as to the accuracy, completeness, timeliness, or reliability of any Content, including any moderation status label shown on it.</p>\n<p>Any decision you make based on Content on the Service \u2014 including a decision to enter a vehicle, meet a person, or otherwise engage with anyone \u2014 is made entirely at your own discretion and risk.</p>\n<h2>11. Moderation \u2014 Our Right, Not an Obligation, to Act</h2>\n<p>Moderation exists to reduce, not eliminate, the risk of false, defamatory, or unlawful Content circulating on the Service.</p>\n<p>The Company may, but is not obliged to: review, label, restrict, redact, remove, or reinstate any Content; suspend or terminate accounts; or take no action at all, notwithstanding a complaint, appeal, or notice it receives.</p>\n<p>Moderation decisions are made by human moderators applying the Company\u2019s internal guidelines. They are not legal, journalistic, or investigative findings, and do not carry the weight of a court or police determination.</p>\n<p>The Company operates a notice-and-response process for complaints about specific Content, consistent with the approach the ECT Act contemplates for service providers \u2014 see Section 17.</p>\n<h2>12. Reported Individuals \u2014 People Who Are Not Users</h2>\n<p>A person who is named or otherwise identified in a Report (a \u201cReported Individual\u201d) is not, by that fact alone, a party to these Terms and has not agreed to them.</p>\n<p>A Reported Individual may submit a correction, dispute, or appeal regarding a Report that concerns them through the process described at <span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[link/appeal mechanism]</span>. The Company may request reasonable proof of identity before acting on such a request, to prevent a Reported Individual\u2019s identity being misused by a third party to suppress a genuine Report.</p>\n<p>Under POPIA, a Reported Individual may have rights as a data subject, including to request access to personal information the Company holds about them, request its correction or deletion, object to its processing on reasonable grounds, and lodge a complaint with the Information Regulator of South Africa. These rights, and how to exercise them, are set out in the Company\u2019s Privacy Policy <span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[link]</span> and are not limited or reduced by anything in these Terms.</p>\n<p>Nothing in these Terms entitles a Reported Individual to obtain the identity of a Reporter who submitted a Report via the Verified Email or Anonymous channel, or of an Account-holder whose identity the Company protects by default, except where the Company is required to disclose it by a valid South African court order, subpoena, or other lawful process, or where the Reporter has consented to disclosure.</p>\n<h2>13. Evidence Submissions</h2>\n<p>You are solely responsible for ensuring you have the legal right to capture, hold, and submit any evidence (photographs, recordings, screenshots, or documents) you upload.</p>\n<p>The lawfulness of recording a conversation or interaction in South Africa can depend on the circumstances of the recording. The Company does not advise on, and takes no position on, the lawfulness of how you obtained any evidence; you upload evidence entirely at your own risk and responsibility.</p>\n<p>The Company may redact, decline to display, or remove evidence at its discretion, including evidence that appears to have been unlawfully obtained, that identifies a third party unnecessarily, or that falls within Section 9 (Prohibited Conduct).</p>\n<h2>14. False Reports, Misuse, and Consequences</h2>\n<p>Knowingly submitting a false Report may expose you to civil liability, including for defamation, and in some circumstances to criminal liability under South African law, including under provisions addressing crimen injuria or the Cybercrimes Act.</p>\n<p>The Company may restrict, remove, or label as \u201cdisputed\u201d or \u201cremoved\u201d any Report found on review to be false, unsubstantiated, or submitted for an improper purpose, and may suspend or terminate the account (where one exists) of a User who submits it.</p>\n<p>Where legally required, or where the Company reasonably believes disclosure is necessary to comply with a court order, prevent harm, or address a legal claim, the Company may disclose information about a Report and its submitter to SAPS, a court, or another authorised body, notwithstanding any other statement in these Terms about identity protection.</p>\n<p>The Company may cooperate with a law-enforcement investigation into suspected false reporting, defamation, or other unlawful use of the Service.</p>\n<h2>15. Intellectual Property</h2>\n<p>Excluding User Content, all rights in the Service \u2014 including its software, design, trademarks, the \u201cUBE Safety\u201d branding, and its text and graphics \u2014 belong to the Company or its licensors.</p>\n<p>You may not copy, modify, reverse-engineer, scrape, or create derivative works from the Service, except as permitted by law or with the Company\u2019s prior written consent.</p>\n<h2>16. Privacy and POPIA</h2>\n<p>The Company processes personal information submitted through the Service, including special personal information concerning alleged criminal behaviour, in accordance with POPIA. Full detail on what is collected, why, and a User\u2019s rights is set out in the Company\u2019s Privacy Policy <span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[link \u2014 to be prepared as a separate document]</span>, which forms part of these Terms.</p>\n<p>The Company acts as the \u201cresponsible party\u201d, as that term is used in POPIA, for personal information processed through the Service.</p>\n<p>POPIA section 57 addresses prior authorisation requirements for processing information concerning alleged criminal behaviour on behalf of third parties in certain circumstances. The Company will not enable a public search feature over Reports until this requirement, and other applicable legal requirements, have been assessed and, if necessary, satisfied.</p>\n<h2>17. Limitation of Liability</h2>\n<p>To the maximum extent permitted by South African law, the Service is provided \u201cas is\u201d and \u201cas available\u201d, without warranty of any kind, whether express or implied, including any implied warranty of merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose, non-infringement, availability, security, or error-free operation.</p>\n<p>To the maximum extent permitted by law, the Company excludes all liability for indirect, special, incidental, consequential, or punitive damages, and for loss of profit, data, goodwill, or opportunity, arising from or related to your use of the Service, even where the Company has been advised of the possibility of such damages.</p>\n<p>To the maximum extent permitted by law, the Company\u2019s total aggregate liability to you for any claim arising from or related to the Service is limited to the greater of (a) the total amount, if any, that you paid the Company for the Service in the 12 months before the claim arose, or (b) ZAR 1,000.</p>\n<p><strong>What this Section does not exclude.</strong> Nothing in these Terms limits or excludes any liability that cannot lawfully be limited or excluded under South African law, including liability for death or personal injury caused by the Company\u2019s negligence, liability for fraud or fraudulent misrepresentation, or liability for the Company\u2019s own gross negligence or wilful misconduct.</p>\n<p>Without limiting the above, the Company is not liable, to the extent permitted by law, for any harm, loss, injury, or damage arising from: (a) a decision you make in reliance on Content, or on the absence of Content, on the Service; (b) the accuracy or inaccuracy of any Report; (c) an interaction, meeting, or transaction between Users or between a User and a third party; or (d) unauthorised access to, alteration of, or loss of Content.</p>\n<h2>18. Indemnification</h2>\n<p>You agree to indemnify, defend, and hold harmless the Company and its directors, employees, and Moderators from and against any claims, damages, losses, liabilities, costs, and expenses (including reasonable legal fees) arising from or related to: (a) Content you submit; (b) your breach of these Terms; (c) your violation of any law or the rights of a third party, including a Reported Individual; or (d) your use of the Service.</p>\n<h2>19. Termination and Suspension</h2>\n<p>The Company may suspend or terminate your access to the Service at any time, with or without notice, including where it suspects a breach of these Terms or considers it necessary for safety, legal, or operational reasons.</p>\n<p>You may stop using the Service, or request deletion of your account, at any time. Certain Content \u2014 including Reports, moderation history, and audit records \u2014 may be retained after account deletion where the Company is required or entitled to retain it, as described in the Privacy Policy.</p>\n<p>Sections of these Terms that by their nature should survive termination \u2014 including Sections 8 (User Content), 10 (No Warranty), 12 (Reported Individuals), 17 (Limitation of Liability), 18 (Indemnification), and 22 (Governing Law) \u2014 continue to apply after your access ends.</p>\n<h2>20. Availability and Changes to the Service</h2>\n<p>The Company does not guarantee that the Service will be available at all times, uninterrupted, or free of errors, and may modify, suspend, or discontinue any part of the Service at any time.</p>\n<h2>21. Changes to These Terms</h2>\n<p>The Company may amend these Terms from time to time. The updated version will be posted with a new \u201cLast Updated\u201d date, and material changes will be flagged through the Service. Your continued use of the Service after a change takes effect constitutes your acceptance of the updated Terms.</p>\n<h2>22. Governing Law and Jurisdiction</h2>\n<p>These Terms are governed by the law of the Republic of South Africa. You and the Company each submit to the non-exclusive jurisdiction of the courts of South Africa in respect of any dispute arising from or related to these Terms or the Service.</p>\n<h2>23. Complaints and Dispute Resolution</h2>\n<p>If you have a complaint about a specific Report or moderation decision, you should first use the appeal/dispute process available within the Service.</p>\n<p>If your complaint concerns the handling of personal information, you may also lodge a complaint with the Information Regulator of South Africa (details available at inforegulator.org.za), in addition to, or instead of, any process available through the Service.</p>\n<p><strong>No mandatory arbitration.</strong> The Company has deliberately not included a clause requiring you to use private arbitration instead of the courts. Many Users of this Service may be dealing with harassment or crime, and the Company does not consider it appropriate to route their access to a remedy through a private process instead of the ordinary courts.</p>\n<h2>24. General Provisions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Severability \u2014 if any provision of these Terms is found unenforceable, the rest continue to apply.</p></li>\n<li><p>No waiver \u2014 the Company\u2019s failure to enforce a provision is not a waiver of its right to do so later.</p></li>\n<li><p>Assignment \u2014 the Company may assign these Terms in connection with a merger, acquisition, or sale of assets; you may not assign your rights under these Terms without the Company\u2019s consent.</p></li>\n<li><p>Entire agreement \u2014 these Terms and the Privacy Policy constitute the entire agreement between you and the Company regarding the Service.</p></li>\n<li><p>Force majeure \u2014 the Company is not liable for a failure or delay caused by circumstances beyond its reasonable control.</p></li>\n<li><p>Relationship of the parties \u2014 nothing in these Terms creates a partnership, agency, employment, or fiduciary relationship between you and the Company.</p></li>\n<li><p>Language \u2014 these Terms are drawn up in English, which is the governing language for their interpretation.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>25. Contact Us</h2>\n<p><span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[Company Legal Name]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[Physical/registered address]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[Support/contact email]</span></p>\n<p>Information Officer (POPIA): [name/role and contact details \u2014 POPIA requires the Company to appoint an Information Officer and register these details with the Information Regulator before this Service processes personal information at scale]</p>\n";

export const PRIVACY_HTML = "<div class=\"ube-legal-masthead\"><p><strong>PRIVACY POLICY</strong></p>\n<p><strong>UBE Safety</strong></p>\n<p>Operated by <span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[Company Legal Name]</span> (Registration No. <span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[\u2022]</span>) (\u201cthe Company\u201d, \u201cwe\u201d, \u201cus\u201d, or \u201cour\u201d)</p>\n<p>Effective Date: 15 September 2026 | Last Updated: 1 September 2026</p>\n<p><strong>DRAFT FOR REVIEW \u2014 NOT YET APPROVED</strong></p>\n<p><em>This document was drafted with AI assistance as a starting point and companion to the Terms of Use. It has not been reviewed by a South African attorney or an Information Officer, and must not be published as the Company's live Privacy Policy until it has been \u2014 in particular the open items on prior authorisation (Section 6) and cross-border transfer (Section 11) below.</em></p>\n</div><h2>1. Who We Are and How to Reach Us</h2>\n<p><span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[Company Legal Name]</span> (\u201cthe Company\u201d, \u201cwe\u201d, \u201cus\u201d, or \u201cour\u201d) operates UBE Safety (the \u201cService\u201d). We are the \u201cresponsible party\u201d, as that term is used in the Protection of Personal Information Act 4 of 2013 (\u201cPOPIA\u201d), for the personal information described in this Privacy Policy.</p>\n<p>Information Officer: <span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[name/role and contact details]</span>. Under POPIA sections 55\u201356, we are required to register our Information Officer's details with the Information Regulator of South Africa before processing personal information through the Service at scale. This is an open item \u2014 see the note on the cover page.</p>\n<p>This Privacy Policy should be read together with our Terms of Use <span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[link]</span>, which governs your use of the Service more broadly.</p>\n<h2>2. Scope \u2014 Whose Information This Policy Covers</h2>\n<p>This Policy covers personal information about several different kinds of people, because of how the Service works:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Reporters \u2014 people who submit a Report, whether as a signed-in Account holder, via the Verified-Email channel, or fully Anonymously.</p></li>\n<li><p>Reported Individuals \u2014 people who are named or identified in someone else's Report. A Reported Individual is not a User of the Service in the ordinary sense and has not created an account or agreed to our Terms, but we still process personal information about them, and this Policy explains how and why.</p></li>\n<li><p>Moderators \u2014 people authorised by the Company to review Content.</p></li>\n<li><p>Anyone who visits the Service, whether or not they submit anything.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>3. What Personal Information We Collect</h2>\n<p><strong>Account holders \u2014</strong> email address, a securely hashed password (we never store your actual password), and any display name you choose to provide.</p>\n<p><strong>Reporters (all three channels) \u2014</strong> the content of your Report (incident category, context, date, general location, and your narrative), whether you indicated the incident was reported to police, a platform, or an employer, and any case/reference number you choose to supply. For the Verified-Email channel, we also collect the email address you provide. For the Anonymous and Verified-Email channels, we store only a one-way cryptographic hash of your recovery code \u2014 never the code itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Reported Individuals \u2014</strong> whatever identifying information a Reporter includes about them in a Report \u2014 for example a name, phone number, social media handle, or vehicle registration \u2014 and any evidence a Reporter uploads that concerns them. This is information we receive from a third party (the Reporter), not directly from the Reported Individual, which affects how some of the rights in Section 9 work in practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Evidence files \u2014</strong> photographs, screenshots, audio, video, or documents uploaded in support of a Report. These are stored in a private, access-controlled location and are never made public; see Section 10.</p>\n<p><strong>Moderators \u2014</strong> account information as above, plus a record of moderation actions taken, including the rationale recorded for each decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Automatically collected information \u2014</strong> timestamps of account and Report activity, a hashed form of your IP address (used only for rate-limiting and abuse prevention \u2014 see Section 8), and basic device/browser information standard to web requests. We do not use this information for advertising or behavioural tracking.</p>\n<h2>4. Special Personal Information</h2>\n<p>POPIA treats certain categories of personal information as \u201cspecial personal information\u201d, subject to stricter rules than ordinary personal information (sections 26\u201327), including information concerning a person's alleged commission of an offence or alleged criminal behaviour (section 33).</p>\n<p>Because the Service exists to let people report alleged harassment, assault, or other unsafe conduct, Reports and the information they contain about a Reported Individual will often constitute special personal information under this definition. We treat this category of information with particular care, including through the following safeguards, which are built into how the Service operates rather than being policy statements alone:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Reports are never published automatically \u2014 every Report is held in a private, moderator-only state until reviewed (see our Terms of Use, Section 11).</p></li>\n<li><p>A Report's moderation status, evidence status, and any legal-process status (for example, a claimed case reference) are tracked as three separate fields, so that \u201ca report exists\u201d is never conflated with \u201cthis was verified\u201d or \u201cthis is a legal finding\u201d.</p></li>\n<li><p>Evidence files are stored in a private location and are only ever accessed through short-lived, individually-issued links, each access to which is logged.</p></li>\n<li><p>A Reported Individual has a route to dispute or appeal a Report that concerns them \u2014 see Section 9.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Open item \u2014 prior authorisation.</strong> POPIA section 57 requires prior authorisation from the Information Regulator before certain processing of information about alleged criminal behaviour on behalf of third parties. The Company has not yet obtained, and \u2014 pending legal advice \u2014 may be required to obtain, this authorisation before operating any feature that goes beyond private, moderator-reviewed storage of Reports (in particular, any public search or lookup feature). No such feature is enabled in the current version of the Service.</p>\n<h2>5. Why We Process This Information</h2>\n<p>We process personal information only for the following purposes, consistent with POPIA's purpose-specification requirement (sections 13\u201314):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>to operate the Report submission, evidence-upload, and moderation features of the Service;</p></li>\n<li><p>to allow a Reporter using the Verified-Email or Anonymous channel to check on their Report later, using their recovery code;</p></li>\n<li><p>to allow a Reported Individual to dispute or appeal a Report concerning them;</p></li>\n<li><p>to detect and prevent abuse of the Service, including false reporting, harassment, and automated or coordinated misuse;</p></li>\n<li><p>to maintain security, audit, and legal-compliance records; and</p></li>\n<li><p>to comply with a legal obligation, or to respond to a valid legal process such as a court order or subpoena.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>We do not process personal information for advertising, do not sell personal information, and do not use it to build advertising profiles.</p>\n<h2>6. Our Legal Basis for Processing</h2>\n<p>Where you create an Account or submit a Report yourself, our basis for processing your information is your consent, given by accepting our Terms of Use, and, where applicable, that processing is necessary to provide the Service you have asked for.</p>\n<p><strong>Reported Individuals.</strong> A Reported Individual has not consented to our processing of their information, because it was submitted by someone else. Our basis for this processing is that it is necessary for the legitimate interests pursued by the Company and by other Users of the Service \u2014 namely, enabling people to make more informed personal-safety decisions \u2014 weighed against the Reported Individual's own rights and interests. This is why the safeguards in Section 4, and the dispute/appeal route in Section 9, matter: they are how we try to keep that balance fair, not just a formality.</p>\n<h2>7. Who We Share Information With</h2>\n<p>We do not sell personal information. We share it only in the following circumstances:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>with Moderators, solely as necessary for them to review and act on Reports;</p></li>\n<li><p>with our infrastructure providers (for example, our database, storage, and hosting providers), acting as operators under POPIA who process information only on our instructions and under contract \u2014 see Section 11 for where this infrastructure is located;</p></li>\n<li><p>with SAPS, a court, or another authorised body, where we are legally required to disclose information, or where we reasonably believe disclosure is necessary to comply with a valid legal process, prevent harm, or address a legal claim; and</p></li>\n<li><p>with a professional adviser (for example, a lawyer or auditor) bound by confidentiality, where necessary.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>8. Automated Processing and Decision-Making</h2>\n<p>Some parts of the Service use automated checks \u2014 for example, rate-limiting based on a hashed identifier to prevent abuse of the Anonymous and Verified-Email reporting channels. These checks affect whether a submission is accepted, not the outcome or credibility of a Report.</p>\n<p>No Report is automatically published, and no automated process determines that a Reported Individual has done anything alleged in a Report. Every consequential moderation decision is made by a human Moderator, who must record a rationale for it. This reflects both our own product principles and the restriction POPIA section 71 places on decisions based solely on automated processing that would affect a person's legal rights or have a similarly significant effect on them.</p>\n<h2>9. Your Rights</h2>\n<p>Subject to POPIA sections 23\u201325, and to the identity-verification step described below, you (and, where a Report concerns you, a Reported Individual) may:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>request confirmation of, and access to, the personal information we hold;</p></li>\n<li><p>request correction of personal information that is inaccurate, incomplete, misleading, or out of date;</p></li>\n<li><p>request deletion or destruction of personal information that we are no longer authorised to hold; and</p></li>\n<li><p>object to our processing of personal information about you, on reasonable grounds relating to your particular situation.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Verifying who is asking.</strong> Because a Reported Individual is often identified by information supplied by someone else, we will ask for reasonable proof of identity before acting on their request. This exists to stop a Reported Individual's identity being used by an unrelated third party to access or suppress a genuine Report \u2014 not to make the process harder than it needs to be.</p>\n<p><strong>Limits for Anonymous and Verified-Email Reporters.</strong> If you submitted a Report anonymously, we generally cannot verify that a later request relates to your Report except through your recovery code, because we hold no other identifying information about you by design.</p>\n<p>To exercise any of these rights, contact our Information Officer using the details in Section 1. You may also lodge a complaint with the Information Regulator of South Africa (details at inforegulator.org.za) at any time.</p>\n<h2>10. How We Store and Protect Information</h2>\n<p>We apply security safeguards consistent with POPIA sections 19\u201322, including the following, which are implemented as actual technical controls rather than policy alone:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>database-level access rules mean a Reporter can only ever read their own Account-channel Reports, and Moderators are the only accounts that can read across all Reports;</p></li>\n<li><p>evidence files are stored in a private location with no public link; every file is only ever retrieved through a short-lived, individually issued link, and access is logged;</p></li>\n<li><p>a recovery code for the Anonymous or Verified-Email channel is never stored \u2014 only a one-way cryptographic hash of it is, meaning even we cannot look up or recover your code;</p></li>\n<li><p>submissions through channels that don't require an account are rate-limited to reduce automated abuse; and</p></li>\n<li><p>data in transit and at rest is encrypted using our infrastructure providers' standard encryption.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>If we become aware of a security compromise that has or may have led to unauthorised access to, or loss of, personal information, we will notify the Information Regulator and affected individuals as soon as reasonably possible, as required by POPIA section 22.</p>\n<h2>11. Where Information Is Stored \u2014 Cross-Border Transfer</h2>\n<p><strong>Open item.</strong> As of this draft, the Service's database and file storage are hosted outside South Africa (in the Company's chosen Supabase region), because no local hosting region is currently available from our infrastructure provider. Personal information \u2014 including special personal information \u2014 is therefore transferred across South Africa's borders in the ordinary course of operating the Service.</p>\n<p>POPIA section 72 restricts transferring personal information outside South Africa unless one of several conditions is met \u2014 for example, the recipient country has adequate data-protection laws, appropriate contractual safeguards are in place with the infrastructure provider, or the data subject has consented to the transfer. Whether the Company's current arrangement satisfies section 72, and what contractual safeguards need to be in place with the infrastructure provider, has not yet been confirmed by a South African attorney and is an open item alongside the section 57 prior-authorisation question in Section 4.</p>\n<h2>12. How Long We Keep Information</h2>\n<p>We retain a Report, its evidence, and its moderation history for as long as necessary for the purposes described in Section 5. As a working default pending legal advice, we intend to retain this information for at least three years after a Report is closed \u2014 broadly aligned with the general prescription period for civil claims in South Africa \u2014 and in any event no longer than 7 years, unless a longer period is required to comply with a legal obligation or an ongoing legal process.</p>\n<p>Where an Account is deleted, some records (for example, audit logs and moderation history) may be retained in de-identified or restricted form as described above, even though the Account itself no longer exists.</p>\n<h2>13. Children</h2>\n<p>The Service is not directed at, and must not be used by, anyone under 18 \u2014 see our Terms of Use, Section 3. We do not knowingly collect personal information from a child, and POPIA sections 34\u201335 place additional restrictions on processing a child's personal information that this Policy does not attempt to authorise. If we become aware that we hold personal information about a child in a manner not permitted by POPIA, we will take steps to delete it.</p>\n<h2>14. Cookies and Similar Technology</h2>\n<p>The Service uses a strictly necessary session cookie to keep you signed in where you have an Account. We do not use third-party advertising or cross-site tracking cookies.</p>\n<h2>15. Changes to This Policy</h2>\n<p>We may update this Policy from time to time. The updated version will be posted with a new \u201cLast Updated\u201d date, and material changes will be flagged through the Service.</p>\n<h2>16. Contact Us</h2>\n<p><span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[Company Legal Name]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[Physical/registered address]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[Support/contact email]</span></p>\n<p>Information Officer: <span class=\"ube-placeholder\">[name/role and contact details]</span></p>\n<p>Information Regulator of South Africa: inforegulator.org.za</p>\n";
